---
import ContentShell from '../../layouts/ContentShell.astro'
import { stats } from '../../data/load'
import { collectionLd } from '../../data/seo'
import { GUIDES, guideUrl } from '../../data/guides'

const s = await stats()

const h1 = 'Guides to the Pune startup job market'
const description =
  'Four written guides to looking for work at a Pune startup: where the offices are, what ' +
  'entry-level roles ask for, how to read a posting, and what each ATS does to your time.'
---

<ContentShell
  title={`${h1} | Punehire`}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/"
  h1={h1}
  lede={description}
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' }]}
  jsonLd={[
    collectionLd(
      h1,
      description,
      GUIDES.map((g) => ({ name: g.title, href: guideUrl(g.slug) })),
      Astro.site,
    ),
  ]}
  narrow
>
  <p>
    The rest of this site is generated. A crawler reads {s.companies} company job feeds every
    six hours and the pages rebuild themselves from whatever came back. These four pages are
    the exception: they are written by hand, and the only numbers in them are pulled from the
    same database at build time so that a guide can never end up contradicting a listing.
  </p>
  <p>
    They are written for someone actually job-hunting in Pune, not for someone reading about
    job-hunting. Where a guide does not know something — what a company pays, whether a team
    really works hybrid — it says so rather than filling the gap.
  </p>

  <div class="grid" style="margin-top:1.6em">
    {GUIDES.map((g) => (
      <a class="tile" href={guideUrl(g.slug)}>
        <h3>{g.nav}</h3>
        <span class="meta">{g.blurb}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 style="margin-top:2.2em">What these are not</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    There is no salary data here. This site reads public job feeds, and Indian startups almost
    never publish a salary band in one, so any figure on this page would be invented. There is
    no company ranking either, and no advice about which employer is a good one to work for —
    that is a judgement the data cannot support. What the guides can do is explain what the
    listings mean, which is a narrower and more honest job.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    Method, sources and known gaps are set out in <a href="/about/">how this works</a>.
  </p>
</ContentShell>
